A||||7||4Banger Bandit||Hey engine guys, help a fellow Fiero owner out!
Z||000000||4Banger Bandit||08-13-99||09:36 AM||||My father and I are getting serious about transplanting a carbed V6 into my '85 2M4, but I've got some questions.  We're going to build the engine from scratch, but I've been told that you need a front-drive V6 to work in the Fiero.  After looking at other posts, it looks like I need a rear-wheel drive block. Can someone please tell me for certain?  I've got the auto tranny, and I'm also wondering what other kinds of things I'm going to need to prepare for to make this swap work.  We plan to go carbed, and do-away with computer and emmisions crap(I live in SD, so it's not an issue.)  I'd appreciate any help.  Thanks guys!  
Z||000001||four50four||08-13-99||10:25 AM||||from what i've heard you need to relocate the starter to the other side rwd blocks.
Z||000002||batboy||08-13-99||01:34 PM||||It's a front wheel drive engine you want. The rear wheel drive blocks require modification to work. You want one with cast iron heads too. The 3.1 with aluminum heads have a special intake manifold which I have not seen any aftermarket carb manifolds being made for this yet. If you are swapping a V6 in place of a 4 cylinder, check with some of the other guys here on the forum. I think you need to change other things too, like motor mounts and fuel pump, etc...
Z||000003||batboy||08-13-99||01:43 PM||||A couple more things... the 1984 and older 2.8 liter engines had smaller diameter crank journals which were generally a little weaker. So 1985 or newer engines are preferred (and necessary if you are stroking it with a 3.1 crank). Also don't get a newer style engine with DIS (which requires a ECM), you want an engine with a distributor, if you are going to run it without a computer.
Z||000004||PaUL||08-13-99||02:46 PM||||what kind of carb are you going to run? If  you are going to stick with stock Rochester carb, youll probably need the ECM that from the donor car. Also after 1980, all 2.8s came with a distributor that was controlled by the ECM. If you dont want to use the ECM, you can do what I did and get an old HEI distributer from an X car. These still had mech and vac adance with no ecm connector. 
Z||000005||4Banger Bandit||08-13-99||04:15 PM||||This is great information guys, thanks. I'm not sure what carb I'm going with, but not Rochester.  I believe my father said he found a Holley that would work well with it.  Batboy, I don't understand why I need a front-drive engine if the Fiero origionally came with a rear-drive engine.  Can someone please explain why?  Also, do you guys know if I would be safe using just the heads off an '84 rear-drive 2.8?  Cuz I already have some.  thanks guys....  
Z||000006||4Banger Bandit||08-13-99||04:33 PM||||quick question I forgot... in 1984, was it just the crank journals that were smaller? could I still use the block itself?? Also, what sort of modifications would it take to make a rear-drive engine work??
Z||000007||batboy||08-13-99||05:34 PM||||The Fiero uses a front wheel drive transaxle and engine set up from a Citation. It just happens to be sitting in the rear. Check out Ed Park's website about converting the rear wheel drive V6/60 degree engines to the Fiero:<p>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/parks/e-conv.htm<p>On the crankshafts, it's the main journals that are larger on the 85 and newer engines. The older style won't self-destruct or anything, it's just that the newer cranks hold up to more abuse. A good machine shop probably could turn down a 3.1 crank to fit an early 2.8 block.<p>Any cast iron heads off any V6/60 degree engine (2.8, 3.1, 3.4) should work all right. Fieros had the HO heads which had larger valves. The high performance heads off of some of the Camaros have even larger exhaust valves.
